\CKN@Section{Compiler Construction Stack}

Since time in memorial, humanity has found that, stacking things up one another creates a strong base for those things on top, and a solid support for those thing at the bottom. From the Mesopotemian\footnote{And Elamite!} zigurats to the Egyptian \& Meso-American pyramids. Humanity finds stacks to be a solid foundation.

\CNK@Box[aside]{%
And the weakest of homes is the \CNK@Hyperlink{https://w3c.org}{home of the spider}, only if they knew?
}


Speaking of webs, when at the early dawn of the World Wide Web, one wanted to build a website, he'd be met with no tools to rely on. It was from this lack of abundance that literal garbage\footnote{This is not a textbook} like PHP rose. At the end of the first decade of the twenty-first century, many were rushing to have their tool stick. It did not work. Open Source won, and most websites today are built upon an entirely libre stack.

Could we bring this notion of an \emph{stack} to compiler construction?

